1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:02,044
Lesson 4.9.

2
00:00:02,424 --> 00:00:03,799
Mean-field theory.

3
00:00:04,917 --> 00:00:06,119
Sampling is expensive.

4
00:00:06,499 --> 00:00:08,448
What if, instead of following each

5
00:00:08,448 --> 00:00:11,315
fluctuating spin, we
followed only its mean value?

6
00:00:11,695 --> 00:00:14,355
That is the idea of
mean field: replace the

7
00:00:14,355 --> 00:00:16,891
neighbors' fluctuations by their average.

8
00:00:17,271 --> 00:00:19,791
The result is a system of deterministic

9
00:00:19,791 --> 00:00:22,635
equations that any
processor solves quickly.

10
00:00:23,015 --> 00:00:27,489
And its form is exactly that of a
neural network with tanh activation.

11
00:00:28,617 --> 00:00:33,176
A pbit with input u_i has mean tanh(βu_i).

12
00:00:33,556 --> 00:00:36,541
But the input depends on the
neighbors, which fluctuate.

13
00:00:36,921 --> 00:00:41,947
Mean field replaces each
neighbor by its mean, s_j → m_j,

14
00:00:42,327 --> 00:00:51,837
and obtains the self-consistent equations
m_i = tanh(β(Σ_j J_ij m_j + h_i)).

15
00:00:52,217 --> 00:00:55,434
We neglect correlations
between neighbors: it is

16
00:00:55,434 --> 00:00:58,785
exact when each spin has
very many weak neighbors.

17
00:00:59,917 --> 00:01:04,710
On a uniform lattice with
z neighbors, all spins have

18
00:01:04,710 --> 00:01:09,233
the same mean and the
equation is m = tanh(βzJ m).

19
00:01:09,613 --> 00:01:14,323
The solution is the intersection of
the line y = m with the tanh curve.

20
00:01:14,703 --> 00:01:18,094
If the slope at the
origin, βzJ, is less than

21
00:01:18,094 --> 00:01:21,185
one, the only solution is zero: disorder.

22
00:01:21,565 --> 00:01:25,968
If it is greater, two symmetric
solutions appear: the system magnetizes.

23
00:01:26,348 --> 00:01:29,541
The transition happens at T_c = zJ.

24
00:01:30,650 --> 00:01:35,918
For the square lattice, with four
neighbors, mean field predicts T_c = 4.

25
00:01:36,298 --> 00:01:39,373
The exact Onsager value is 2.27.

26
00:01:39,753 --> 00:01:42,157
Mean field overestimates order, because it

27
00:01:42,157 --> 00:01:44,504
ignores the fluctuations that destroy it.

28
00:01:44,884 --> 00:01:51,587
At temperature two it predicts a
magnetization of 0.96, versus 0.91 exact.

29
00:01:51,967 --> 00:01:55,203
And at temperature three
it predicts 0.78, when

30
00:01:55,203 --> 00:01:58,232
in reality the system
is already disordered.

31
00:01:59,350 --> 00:02:05,907
Let's solve the equations by
iteration: m ← tanh(β(J m + h)).

32
00:02:06,287 --> 00:02:09,589
Each iteration is a
neural network layer: a

33
00:02:09,589 --> 00:02:12,431
matrix, a bias and a tanh activation.

34
00:02:12,811 --> 00:02:16,869
With T = 2, starting
from 0.5, the iteration

35
00:02:16,869 --> 00:02:20,559
converges to 0.957 in about five layers.

36
00:02:20,939 --> 00:02:25,251
So a pbit network that samples and
a deterministic neural network are

37
00:02:25,251 --> 00:02:29,250
two faces of the same model: one
fluctuates, the other averages.

38
00:02:30,350 --> 00:02:32,422
There is a deeper justification.

39
00:02:32,802 --> 00:02:36,910
Mean field searches, among all
distributions that factorize spin by

40
00:02:36,910 --> 00:02:40,834
spin, for the one that minimizes
the variational free energy, or

41
00:02:40,834 --> 00:02:44,636
equivalently, the KL divergence
to the Boltzmann distribution.

42
00:02:45,016 --> 00:02:48,016
By the Gibbs–Bogoliubov
inequality, that free

43
00:02:48,016 --> 00:02:51,217
energy is always an upper
bound on the true one.

44
00:02:51,597 --> 00:02:55,601
It is the same idea as variational
inference in machine learning.

45
00:02:56,717 --> 00:03:02,025
Mean field gives a fast approximation of
the model phase for training Boltzmann

46
00:03:02,025 --> 00:03:07,065
machines, and explains the tanh units
we will build from pbits in unit ten.

47
00:03:07,445 --> 00:03:11,095
Belief propagation and
TAP corrections refine it.

48
00:03:11,475 --> 00:03:14,396
It is exact with
infinite-range interactions and

49
00:03:14,396 --> 00:03:17,014
poor near the transition
in low dimensions.

50
00:03:18,117 --> 00:03:23,270
Common mistakes: trusting the mean-field
critical temperature in two dimensions.

51
00:03:23,650 --> 00:03:27,602
Taking the zero solution below
the transition, which is unstable.

52
00:03:27,982 --> 00:03:29,985
Forgetting that it ignores correlations.

53
00:03:30,365 --> 00:03:33,392
And iterating without
damping, which can oscillate.

54
00:03:34,517 --> 00:03:39,526
In summary: mean field
replaces the neighbors by their

55
00:03:39,526 --> 00:03:43,886
means and gets m_i =
tanh(β(Σ J_ij m_j + h_i)).

56
00:03:44,266 --> 00:03:48,954
It predicts a transition at T_c
= zJ, which overestimates order.

57
00:03:49,334 --> 00:03:52,794
Iterating it means applying
tanh layers: the bridge between

58
00:03:52,794 --> 00:03:56,021
sampling with pbits and
computing with neural networks.

59
00:03:57,150 --> 00:04:00,529
To practice: solve the
self-consistent equation.

60
00:04:00,909 --> 00:04:03,566
Derive the behavior near the transition.

61
00:04:03,946 --> 00:04:06,800
Compute the critical temperature
of the triangular lattice.

62
00:04:07,180 --> 00:04:10,979
And explain why mean field
is exact with infinite range.
